      "observed": {
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "text": "Studying electromagnetic induction and the magnetic properties of matter does not complete our study of electrodynamics.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 107.734375,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 109.125
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "text": "Earlier, we mainly studied electrostatic fields produced by stationary charges and magnetostatic fields produced by steady currents or other stable magnetic sources.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 233.8125,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 145.5
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "text": "The charges in a current move, but a magnetostatic field is defined by not changing with time, not by an individual charge moving at constant velocity.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 396.265625,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 145.5
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "text": "Electromagnetic induction introduced us to time-varying electromagnetic fields.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 558.71875,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 72.75
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "text": "Alternating electromagnetic phenomena and high-frequency electromagnetic phenomena remain to be studied, and they are important in practical applications.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 648.421875,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 145.5
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "text": "Next, we can use the basic laws of electrodynamics already studied to learn about the properties, generation and uses of alternating current.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 810.875,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 145.5
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 6,
            "text": "We will also explore a basic property of electromagnetic fields: a time-varying electric field can also produce a magnetic field, corresponding to the induction of an electric field by a changing magnetic field.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 973.328125,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 181.875
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 7,
            "text": "We can then build on this understanding to study high-frequency electromagnetic fields and electromagnetic waves.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 1172.15625,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 109.125
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          },
          {
            "index": 8,
            "text": "Connecting these topics will further develop our understanding of electrodynamics and give us a more complete picture of electromagnetic processes in nature.",
            "rect": {
              "x": 16,
              "y": 1298.234375,
              "width": 358,
              "height": 145.5
            },
            "section": false,
            "border": "0px",
            "indent": "0px"
          }
        ],
